  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 9, $j$3.1 Sample Space and Probability$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Proverbs 16 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Proverbs/16"}], "body": "Probability measures how likely an event is to happen. Christians believe that nothing is outside the control of God. Proverbs 16:33 says, The lot is cast into the lap; but the whole disposal thereof is of the LORD. Probability does not say that things happen without God. It is a tool that helps us describe, in numbers, what we can and cannot predict. We use it to plan wisely, and we do not use it to chase fortune or to excuse carelessness.\n\nA random process is one whose individual outcomes are uncertain but which shows a regular pattern over many repetitions. An outcome is one possible result, and the sample space is the list of all possible outcomes. An event is a set of outcomes we care about. For a single die, the sample space is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6. The event of rolling an even number is 2, 4 and 6.\n\nWhen all outcomes are equally likely, the probability of an event equals the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes. The probability of rolling an even number is 3 out of 6, which is 1/2, 0.5 or 50 percent. Every probability is a number from 0 to 1. A probability of 0 means the event is impossible and a probability of 1 means it is certain. The probabilities of all outcomes in a sample space add to 1.\n\nFor two dice there are 6 times 6, or 36, equally likely outcomes. There are six ways to roll a sum of 7: 1 and 6, 2 and 5, 3 and 4, 4 and 3, 5 and 2, and 6 and 1. So the probability of a sum of 7 is 6/36, which simplifies to 1/6.\n\nTheoretical probability comes from reasoning about equally likely outcomes. Experimental probability comes from observing what actually happens: the number of times an event occurs divided by the number of trials. The law of large numbers says that as the number of trials grows, the experimental probability tends to move toward the theoretical probability. A short run can look very different from the long run pattern.\n\nYour task: (1) List the sample space for tossing two coins. (2) Find the probability of a sum of 7 with two dice. (3) Explain the difference between theoretical and experimental probability.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.DP.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.DP", "script": "Welcome back. Listen, Proverbs 16:33 says, The lot is cast into the lap; but the whole disposal thereof is of the LORD. Probability does not say that things happen without God. It is a tool to describe in numbers what we can and cannot predict, and we use it to plan wisely.\n\nNow watch this. The sample space is the list of all possible outcomes. For one die it is 1 through 6. An event is the set of outcomes we care about, like rolling an even number, which is 2, 4 and 6.\n\nWhen outcomes are equally likely, the probability of an event is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes. The chance of an even number is 3 out of 6, which is 1/2. Every probability is between 0 and 1. Zero is impossible. One is certain. And all the outcome probabilities add to 1.\n\nLook at two dice. There are 36 equally likely outcomes. Six of them add to 7, so the probability of a sum of 7 is 6/36, which is 1/6.\n\nTheoretical probability comes from reasoning. Experimental probability comes from what actually happens. The law of large numbers says that over many trials the experimental probability moves toward the theoretical one.\n\nYour task is to list a sample space, find a probability and explain the two kinds of probability. Take the four question check. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.1 Sample Space and Probability$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 10, $j$3.2 Complements and the Addition Rule$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Matthew 10 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Matthew/10"}], "body": "Sometimes it is easier to find the chance that something does not happen. Jesus taught in Matthew 10:29 and 30, Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing? and one of them shall not fall on the ground without your Father. But the very hairs of your head are all numbered. The God who numbers hairs is the God behind every ordered pattern, and we count carefully because careful counting is part of good stewardship.\n\nThe complement of an event A is the event that A does not happen. The probability of the complement equals 1 minus the probability of A. If the chance of rolling a 6 is 1/6, then the chance of not rolling a 6 is 5/6. This is useful for phrases such as at least one. The chance of at least one head in three tosses of a coin equals 1 minus the chance of no heads. The chance of no heads is 1/2 times 1/2 times 1/2, which is 1/8, so the chance of at least one head is 7/8.\n\nTwo events are mutually exclusive, or disjoint, if they cannot happen at the same time. On one roll of a die, rolling a 2 and rolling a 3 are mutually exclusive. For mutually exclusive events, the probability of A or B is the probability of A plus the probability of B. So the chance of rolling a 2 or a 3 is 1/6 plus 1/6, which is 2/6, or 1/3.\n\nIf events can happen together, we must not count the overlap twice. The general addition rule states that P of A or B equals P of A plus P of B minus P of A and B. Consider a deck of 52 cards with 13 hearts and 4 aces. One card, the ace of hearts, is both. The probability of drawing a heart or an ace is 13/52 plus 4/52 minus 1/52, which is 16/52, or 4/13.\n\nA Venn diagram can help. Draw two overlapping circles. The overlap shows what belongs to both events. Counting the overlap once is the key.\n\nYour task: (1) Find the probability of a complement. (2) Decide whether two events are mutually exclusive. (3) Use the addition rule to find the probability of drawing a heart or an ace.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.DP.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.DP", "script": "Welcome back. Listen, Matthew 10:30 says, But the very hairs of your head are all numbered. The God who numbers hairs is behind every ordered pattern, and careful counting is part of good stewardship.\n\nNow watch this. The complement of an event is the event that it does not happen. The probability of the complement is 1 minus the probability of the event. The chance of rolling a 6 is 1/6, so the chance of not rolling a 6 is 5/6. This helps with the phrase at least one. The chance of at least one head in three tosses is 1 minus the chance of no heads. No heads is 1/8, so at least one head is 7/8.\n\nLook at the addition rule. Two events are mutually exclusive when they cannot happen together. Rolling a 2 or a 3 on one roll of a die is 1/6 plus 1/6, which is 1/3.\n\nWhen events can overlap, do not count the overlap twice. P of A or B equals P of A plus P of B minus P of A and B. In a deck of 52, there are 13 hearts and 4 aces, and the ace of hearts is both. So the chance of a heart or an ace is 13 plus 4 minus 1, over 52, which is 16/52, or 4/13.\n\nA Venn diagram with two overlapping circles shows the overlap clearly.\n\nYour task is to find a complement, decide whether events are mutually exclusive and use the addition rule. Take the four question check. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.2 Complements and the Addition Rule$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 11, $j$3.3 Independent Events and the Multiplication Rule$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Ecclesiastes 9 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Ecclesiastes/9"}], "body": "Often we want the chance that two things both happen. Ecclesiastes 9:11 observes, I returned, and saw under the sun, that the race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong, neither yet bread to the wise, nor yet riches to men of understanding, nor yet favour to men of skill; but time and chance happeneth to them all. Solomon teaches humility. We can study chance, but we cannot control every outcome.\n\nTwo events are independent if knowing that one happened does not change the probability of the other. Tossing a coin twice gives independent events. The first toss does not influence the second. For independent events, the probability that both happen equals the product of the probabilities: P of A and B equals P of A times P of B. The chance of two heads in two tosses is 1/2 times 1/2, which is 1/4. The chance of rolling a 6 and then another 6 on a die is 1/6 times 1/6, which is 1/36. The chance of a head on a coin and an even number on a die is 1/2 times 1/2, which is 1/4.\n\nSome events are dependent. The outcome of the first changes the chances for the second. This happens when we draw without replacement. A bag holds 5 red marbles and 3 blue marbles. If we draw two marbles without putting the first back, the chance the first is red is 5/8. After one red is gone, 4 red remain out of 7 marbles, so the chance the second is also red is 4/7. The probability of two reds is 5/8 times 4/7, which is 20/56, or 5/14.\n\nTo check independence, ask whether the chance of the second event is the same no matter what happened first. Many people make the gambler's mistake. They think a coin that has landed heads five times is due for tails. The coin has no memory. The next toss is still 1/2.\n\nYour task: (1) Use the multiplication rule for independent events. (2) Find the probability of drawing two reds without replacement. (3) Explain why a coin is never due for tails.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.DP.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.DP", "script": "Welcome back. Listen, Ecclesiastes 9:11 says, time and chance happeneth to them all. Solomon is teaching humility. We can study chance, but we cannot control every outcome.\n\nNow watch this. Two events are independent if knowing that one happened does not change the chance of the other. Tossing a coin twice gives independent events. For independent events, the chance that both happen is the product. The chance of two heads is 1/2 times 1/2, which is 1/4. The chance of a 6 and then another 6 is 1/36.\n\nLook at dependent events. A bag has 5 red and 3 blue marbles. We draw two without putting the first back. The chance the first is red is 5/8. Now 4 reds remain out of 7, so the chance the second is red is 4/7. The chance of two reds is 5/8 times 4/7, which is 20/56, or 5/14.\n\nTo check independence, ask whether the chance of the second event stays the same no matter what happened first.\n\nAnd avoid the gambler's mistake. A coin that landed heads five times in a row is not due for tails. The coin has no memory. The next toss is still 1/2.\n\nYour task is to use the multiplication rule, find a probability without replacement and explain why a coin is never due. Take the four question check. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.3 Independent Events and the Multiplication Rule$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 12, $j$3.4 Conditional Probability$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "James 4 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/James/4"}], "body": "Conditional probability is the chance of an event given that another event has already occurred. James 4:13 to 15 warns against making plans as if we control tomorrow, and says, For that ye ought to say, If the Lord will, we shall live, and do this, or that. Conditional language is wise language. We often make plans that depend on a condition.\n\nThe notation P of A given B means the probability of A when we know B happened. When we know B, our sample space shrinks to B. The formula is that P of A given B equals P of A and B, divided by P of B.\n\nA two way table organizes this. A survey of 80 students recorded who sings in the choir and who plays an instrument. Twenty students do both, 30 sing in the choir only, 10 play an instrument only, and 20 do neither. The total in the choir is 50 and the total who play an instrument is 30.\n\nThe probability that a randomly chosen student sings in the choir and plays an instrument is 20/80, or 1/4. What if we know the student sings in the choir? Then we look only at the 50 choir members. The probability of playing an instrument given choir is 20/50, which is 2/5. Compare that with the opposite question. The probability of being in the choir given the student plays an instrument is 20/30, which is 2/3. The two conditional probabilities are different. Order matters.\n\nEvents are independent if and only if P of A given B equals P of A. Here the chance of playing an instrument for all students is 30/80, or 3/8, which is 0.375. The chance of playing given choir is 0.4. These are not equal, so being in choir and playing an instrument are slightly associated in this group.\n\nThe multiplication rule in general is P of A and B equals P of A times P of B given A. That is how we found the marble problem in the last lesson.\n\nYour task: (1) Compute a conditional probability from a two way table. (2) Explain why P of A given B is not the same as P of B given A. (3) Tell how to test whether two events are independent.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.DP.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.DP", "script": "Welcome back. Listen, James 4:15 says, For that ye ought to say, If the Lord will, we shall live, and do this, or that. Conditional language is wise language.\n\nNow watch this. Conditional probability is the chance of an event given that another event has already occurred. P of A given B equals P of A and B, divided by P of B. When we know B happened, our sample space shrinks to B.\n\nLook at the table. We surveyed 80 students. Twenty sing in the choir and play an instrument. Thirty sing in the choir only. Ten play an instrument only. Twenty do neither. The choir has 50 members. Thirty students play an instrument.\n\nThe chance a student is in the choir and plays an instrument is 20/80, or 1/4. Given the student is in the choir, we look only at 50 students, so the chance of playing an instrument is 20/50, or 2/5. Given the student plays an instrument, the chance of being in the choir is 20/30, or 2/3. The two conditional probabilities are different. Order matters.\n\nEvents are independent if P of A given B equals P of A. Overall, 30 of 80 play an instrument, which is 0.375. Among choir members it is 0.4. They are not equal, so choir and instrument are slightly associated.\n\nYour task is to compute a conditional probability, explain why order matters and test for independence. Take the four question check. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$3.4 Conditional Probability$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'assessment', 3, $j$Assessment 3: The Language of Probability$j$, $j${"prompt": "Write a report of at least 250 words on probability. Use complete sentences, show every calculation as a fraction and label each part. Proverbs 16:33 says, The lot is cast into the lap; but the whole disposal thereof is of the LORD.\n\nPart 1: List the sample space for tossing a coin and rolling a die together, and find the probability of a head and an even number.\nPart 2: Find the probability of not rolling a 6 on a die, and the probability of at least one head in four tosses of a coin.\nPart 3: Use the addition rule to find the probability of drawing a heart or a king from a standard deck of 52 cards.\nPart 4: A bag holds 4 red and 6 blue marbles. Find the probability of drawing two reds with replacement and without replacement, and explain why they differ.\nPart 5: In a class of 60 students, 36 play a sport, 24 are in a band, and 12 do both. Make a two way table and find the probability that a student is in the band given that the student plays a sport. Explain, in your own words, why probability helps us plan but does not replace trust in God.", "rubric": "100 points. Part 1: 15 points. Part 2: 20 points. Part 3: 15 points. Part 4: 20 points. Part 5: 25 points. Complete sentences and conventions: 5 points. A score of 70 or above is passing."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'assessment' and title = $j$Assessment 3: The Language of Probability$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'discussion', 3, $j$Discussion 3: Chance, Planning and Trust$j$, $j${"prompt": "Write a post of at least 120 words that (1) describes a decision you or your family made by weighing chances, such as whether to travel in bad weather or buy insurance, (2) explains the difference between wise planning and trusting luck, (3) describes how the gambler's mistake could lead a person to bad choices, and (4) connects your answer to Proverbs 16:33, Ecclesiastes 9:11 or James 4:15. Then write a respectful reply of at least 40 words to another student's post, or answer this question: How can a believer use probability without forgetting that God is in control? Keep your tone kind and respectful of other views."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'discussion' and title = $j$Discussion 3: Chance, Planning and Trust$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 11, $j$Check 3.1: Sample Space and Probability$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "The probability of rolling a number greater than 4 on a fair die is:", "options": ["2/3", "1/6", "1/3", "1/2"]}, {"q": "A probability can never be:", "options": ["Equal to 1/2", "Equal to 0", "Greater than 1", "Equal to 1"]}, {"q": "The number of equally likely outcomes when rolling two dice is:", "options": ["12", "6", "36", "24"]}, {"q": "The law of large numbers says that over many trials:", "options": ["Theory is always wrong", "Probability becomes zero", "Every outcome happens equally often in ten trials", "Experimental probability tends toward theoretical probability"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.1: Sample Space and Probability$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,2,2,3}'::int[], array[$j$The favorable outcomes are 5 and 6, so 2 out of 6 equals 1/3.$j$,$j$Probabilities range from 0 to 1.$j$,$j$6 times 6 equals 36.$j$,$j$Long run frequencies settle near the true probability.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.1: Sample Space and Probability$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 12, $j$Check 3.2: Complements and the Addition Rule$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "If the chance of rain is 0.3, the chance of no rain is:", "options": ["0.03", "0.7", "0.3", "1.3"]}, {"q": "The chance of at least one head in three coin tosses is:", "options": ["7/8", "1/8", "3/8", "1/2"]}, {"q": "The probability of drawing a heart or an ace from a standard deck is:", "options": ["13/52", "17/52", "4/52", "16/52"]}, {"q": "Two events that cannot happen at the same time are:", "options": ["Certain", "Mutually exclusive", "Complements always", "Independent"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.2: Complements and the Addition Rule$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{1,0,3,1}'::int[], array[$j$The complement is 1 minus 0.3.$j$,$j$One minus the chance of no heads, which is 1/8.$j$,$j$13 plus 4 minus 1 equals 16, over 52.$j$,$j$That is the definition of mutually exclusive.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.2: Complements and the Addition Rule$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 13, $j$Check 3.3: Independent Events and the Multiplication Rule$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "The probability of two heads in two fair coin tosses is:", "options": ["1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2"]}, {"q": "Drawing without replacement makes the events:", "options": ["Independent", "Mutually exclusive always", "Dependent", "Impossible"]}, {"q": "A bag has 5 red and 3 blue marbles. Drawing two without replacement, P(two reds) is:", "options": ["5/14", "25/64", "1/4", "5/8"]}, {"q": "After five heads in a row, the chance of heads on the next toss of a fair coin is:", "options": ["1/32", "1", "1/2", "0"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.3: Independent Events and the Multiplication Rule$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,2,0,2}'::int[], array[$j$1/2 times 1/2 equals 1/4.$j$,$j$The first draw changes the contents of the bag.$j$,$j$5/8 times 4/7 equals 20/56, which is 5/14.$j$,$j$The coin has no memory, so it is still 1/2.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.3: Independent Events and the Multiplication Rule$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 14, $j$Check 3.4: Conditional Probability$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "P of A given B means:", "options": ["The probability of neither", "The probability of A or B", "The probability of B given A", "The probability of A when B is known to have happened"]}, {"q": "In the choir survey, P(instrument given choir) is:", "options": ["20/80", "30/80", "20/30", "20/50"]}, {"q": "In the choir survey, P(choir given instrument) is:", "options": ["20/30", "50/80", "10/30", "20/50"]}, {"q": "Events A and B are independent if:", "options": ["P(A or B) equals 1", "P(A) equals P(B)", "A and B never happen", "P(A given B) equals P(A)"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.4: Conditional Probability$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,3,0,3}'::int[], array[$j$A given B restricts attention to the case where B occurred.$j$,$j$Twenty of the 50 choir members play an instrument.$j$,$j$Twenty of the 30 instrument players sing in choir.$j$,$j$Knowing B does not change the chance of A.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 3.4: Conditional Probability$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 15, $j$Unit Quiz 3: The Language of Probability$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "The sample space for tossing two coins has how many outcomes?", "options": ["6", "2", "4", "3"]}, {"q": "The probability of rolling a sum of 7 with two dice is:", "options": ["1/7", "1/12", "7/36", "1/6"]}, {"q": "If P(A) equals 0.4, then P(not A) equals:", "options": ["0.6", "1.4", "0.04", "0.4"]}, {"q": "For mutually exclusive events A and B, P(A or B) equals:", "options": ["P(A) plus P(B)", "P(A) times P(B)", "1", "P(A) minus P(B)"]}, {"q": "The probability of rolling a 6 twice in a row is:", "options": ["2/6", "1/6", "1/12", "1/36"]}, {"q": "Drawing two cards from a deck without replacement gives:", "options": ["Independent events", "Certain events", "Mutually exclusive events always", "Dependent events"]}, {"q": "In the choir survey, P(choir and instrument) is:", "options": ["50/80", "20/80", "20/30", "20/50"]}, {"q": "A fair coin lands heads six times in a row. The chance of tails next is:", "options": ["Greater than 1/2", "Less than 1/2", "1/2", "1"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 3: The Language of Probability$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,3,0,0,3,3,1,2}'::int[], array[$j$HH, HT, TH and TT.$j$,$j$Six of 36 outcomes, which simplifies to 1/6.$j$,$j$One minus 0.4 is 0.6.$j$,$j$With no overlap, simply add.$j$,$j$1/6 times 1/6 equals 1/36.$j$,$j$The first card changes the deck.$j$,$j$Twenty of the 80 students do both.$j$,$j$Each toss is independent.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 3: The Language of Probability$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
